# actors — commit author to harness, for authors the git graph cannot vote on.
#
# cutover.sh derives the Gas Town roster from the graph itself: every `polecat/<name>/` branch
# ref and every "Merge ... polecat/<name>/..." subject names a polecat, and those refs and
# subjects are permanent, so a polecat that commits tomorrow classifies itself and this file
# stays empty for it. The same idea as prefix-map, and for the same reason — derive what can be
# derived, and keep a file only for what cannot.
#
# Two things the graph cannot say:
#
#   - THE OPERATOR. Nothing in a commit distinguishes a human from a machine, and on a
#     single-user box both sign with the same address. They have to be named.
#   - An identity that appears once and never again. `other` is the honest answer for those and
#     they should usually stay there.
#
# Format: <author-name>=<spira|gastown|human|other>, one per line, `#` comments.
#
# DO NOT ADD A NAME HERE TO MAKE THE VERDICT PASS. An unclassified author is only ever an
# obstacle when it is DECISIVE — `cutover.sh verdict` computes the comparison twice, once
# crediting every unknown to Gas Town and once to Spira, and reports undecided only when those
# two disagree. So a line here is a claim about who somebody is, and nothing else.

# THESE ROWS ARE EXAMPLES. Copy this file to `actors` beside it and write your own; the rows
# below name identities that are not yours. An operator's session, their statute synthesiser
# and their generators may all commit under one name, so `human` means "neither harness"
# rather than "typed by a person", and the report says so.
#
#   alex=human
#   mayor=gastown
#   deacon=gastown
